                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d2e22d74-43a2-3efd-af5e-b638fbd6ac68",
                        "entry_id": "811d4c35-7899-33d4-8122-800fa54087f4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Name eines Gewässers (Fluss, See, Meer, …)"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "23ab4330-fa24-4337-a367-78f81db1e192",
                                "sense_id": "d2e22d74-43a2-3efd-af5e-b638fbd6ac68",
                                "text": "„Saale“, „Mittelmeer“ und „Aralsee“ sind Hydronyme."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02bb2eec-0647-4f60-9615-c19d2b9a1040",
                                "sense_id": "d2e22d74-43a2-3efd-af5e-b638fbd6ac68",
                                "text": "Hydronyme als linguistische Einheit sind meist sehr dauerhaft; nachrückende Völker behalten den ursprünglichen Namen eines Flusses, eines Sees, eines Meeresabschnitts oft bei."
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "908ae7f3-f061-4884-b940-7003d049471f",
                        "source_entry_id": "811d4c35-7899-33d4-8122-800fa54087f4",
                        "source_sense_id": "d2e22d74-43a2-3efd-af5e-b638fbd6ac68",
                        "target_lang_word": "hydronym",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "hydronym",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d35d5425-c726-301c-80c3-2a6dc8dec958",
                                        "entry_id": "ad3378de-df67-3c25-9d0e-9c6919fca522",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The name of a river, lake, sea or any other body of water."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b21391c1-0d37-407d-b62f-e0a250b37d0a",
                                                "sense_id": "d35d5425-c726-301c-80c3-2a6dc8dec958",
                                                "text": "Numerous hydronyms of Asia Minor, attested in the historical Anatolian languages and going back to Proto-Anatolian, can be compared to the Indo-European derivational type of hydronyms in *-ntʰ-. […] Another hydronym of Proto-Anatolian origin and having wide Indo-European connections is Alda, a river in Asia Minor […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6c5cd963-4190-4e84-9518-e074eeffdc5f",
                                                "sense_id": "d35d5425-c726-301c-80c3-2a6dc8dec958",
                                                "text": "Dutch Zijpe, Zijp (and regional variants) is found in toponyms, e.g. the poulder De/Het Zijpe in the province of North-Holland, the name of the castle Sijpestein (Nieuw-Loosdrecht), […] We are dealing with an old hydronym."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "91dd5e0b-6207-42ca-b156-d3691779bd32",
                                                "sense_id": "d35d5425-c726-301c-80c3-2a6dc8dec958",
                                                "text": "Considering the fact that the Yeneseic languages are historically spoken along almost the entire length of the Yenesei from the Minusinsk basin to the Arctic zone it is curious that no Common Yeneseic name for the river seems to have been preserved in the linguistic material. […] A hydronym that possibly belongs to this context is Kezes, which has been derived from Ket (*)qä-ses 'large water' […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d56abab6-4f9c-471c-acd8-c11028ff8bb2",
                                                "sense_id": "d35d5425-c726-301c-80c3-2a6dc8dec958",
                                                "text": "A river name is a hydronym, a name of a body of water. The whole stock of hydronyms in a district, territory, country, or the like is called its hydronymy."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
